  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 3-year-old boy, including fatigue, fever, pallor, cervical lymphadenopathy, petechiae, and splenomegaly, is indicative of a hematological disorder. The laboratory findings show anemia (hemoglobin 9.3 g/dL), leukocytosis (63,000/mm3), and thrombocytopenia (30,000/mm3). The bone marrow aspirate reveals predominantly immature leukocytes that are positive for CD10, CD19, and terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), while being negative for myeloperoxidase. This immunophenotype is consistent with Pre-B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), as CD10 and CD19 are markers for B-cell lineage, and the negativity for myeloperoxidase rules out myeloid leukemias. The reasoning chains correctly lead to the diagnosis of Pre-B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, supported by the consensus that these findings align with this specific type of leukemia in pediatric patients."
